3. RULINGS ON THE BUILDER'S FIVE DISCLOSURES
=============================================================================

D1 — "for behaviours 7-9 I briefly wrote Board.tsx's real container logic ahead of
     schedule — caught it, reverted to a skeleton, re-recorded properly, then restored".
     RULING: THE SAME CLASS AS W-071. Not distinguishable, and it is not mine to waive
     a second time.
     Mechanism, compared side by side: D-024 records W-071 as "the builder implemented
     all five behaviours, verified green, stubbed each piece to a no-op, recorded the
     five reds against the stubbed states, and restored". W-064's sequence is
     implement -> revert to skeleton -> record the red against the skeleton -> restore.
     The same act, the same artifact signature (studio/ci/reds/W-064/07..09.log carry
     dirty:c032fb5b, byte-identical to the eight reds recorded without any such
     detour — P-012 names exactly this: "the header names the committed HEAD, never
     the working tree's content"), and the same verifiability: by candour, not by
     design. Degree differs ("briefly", one module group, self-caught); the class does
     not. The brief left no room to read it otherwise — *Recording the reds* calls it
     "forbidden here whether or not it is detectable", and rev 3 wrote that sentence
     BECAUSE of D-024.
     Credit, on the same terms D-024 set: the builder disclosed it unprompted and in
     full, and that is why a ruling is possible at all. The reds' own shape is sound
     (section 2), so this finding is not what fails the round — B1-B8 are.
     WHAT FOLLOWS, and this is the part that is not discretionary: D-024's kill_when
     has two arms, and the SECOND one has now fired — "or a second waiver of this
     class is requested before that mechanism lands, which is the pattern this decision
     exists to prevent". P-012 (working-tree identity in `bisellium red`) has not
     landed; this is the second instance. So D-024 does not stretch over W-064, and I
     do not extend it: a waiver needs a decision id and the Patron's hand
     (`bisellium waive --decision <id>`), and my own lex forbids adopting a blocking
     rule alone (§4). Recorded here as a second live instance of the class, which is
     the fact P-012 was waiting for. Round 2 must cite D-024 or a new Patron decision
     for the red gate rather than passing it silently — D-024's own requirement, now
     inherited by this opus.

D2 — two test-assertion bugs (a boardview.test.ts count regex, a usage.test.ts
     sentence regex) fixed after their reds were recorded, before implementation.
     RULING: ACCEPTED, with the gap named. Checked against the artifacts: 05.log's
     assertion NAMES are the shipped ones ("BoardView: In progress count is \"1\"" is
     present and FAILing, with the old regex's detail column `>`), and 11.log carries
     all five shipped behaviour-11 assertion names as FAILs. So the shipped assertions
     were observed red in substance. What has no red is the corrected REGEX itself.
     That is the honest cost of fixing a test after step 3 of *The exact sequence*;
     it is small here because both fixes made the assertion stricter (the count regex
     now anchors on the column-head wrapper so it cannot match the phone strip —
     boardview.test.ts:71-76), and neither is among the escapes in section 1.

D3 — a Node loader hook stubbing .css imports (apps/web/test/support/).
     RULING: ACCEPTED. Test scaffolding, mine to allow (lex §2), and it is the smallest
     thing that works: GateLadder.tsx is another opus's file and imports w025.css
     unconditionally, so behaviour 5(a)'s byte-identity proof cannot execute the real
     component under plain node without it. It cannot affect the proof —
     renderToStaticMarkup output does not depend on a stylesheet, and the hook only
     ever runs under `node --import` (css-stub-hook.mjs:1-17). Both files carry their
     own why. Not named in Files owned; forced by a file this opus is forbidden to
     change, which is the right trade.

D4 — behaviour 7's reconnect cases use kill-and-restart-on-the-same-port instead of
     browserContext.setOffline.
     RULING: ACCEPTED, and it is the stronger harness. Verified it genuinely severs the
     stream rather than passing anyway: M10b (drop the drawer's events from
     reconciliation) makes the open-drawer reconnect case FAIL after 15.7s, which only
     happens if the client really disconnected, really reconnected, and really
     reconciled. The claim that setOffline is a no-op on an established loopback SSE
     stream is consistent with what I observed and is recorded in the spec's own header
     (board.spec.ts:43-56) with its measurement. It touches nothing W-067 owns —
     harness.ts and playwright.serve.config.ts are unmodified (git diff confirms).

D5 — a pre-existing gitignored .bisellium cache under examples/sample-studio cleaned,
     freshStudio() hardened.
     RULING: ACCEPTED, and it is the right fix in the right place:
     apps/server/test/board.test.ts:55-68 strips .bisellium from every temp copy, with
     the measurement that motivated it recorded inline ("a leftover snapshot doubled
     the ingested event count"). This is a real hazard for any test that copies the
     fixture, and the hardening belongs in the copy helper rather than in a cleanup
     step someone forgets.

D6 — NOT disclosed, recorded by me: the attended smoke in *Acceptance* asks the censor
     to drive a real browser by hand (open a card, traverse, watch a card move, kill
     the server). I ran the scripted equivalent (section 2) against a temp copy of
